# No `Co-authored-by: Cursor` trailer on commits

When creating git commits in this repository (or any repository), do **NOT** add the following trailer (or any similar variant) to the commit message:

```
Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>
```

The user has explicitly asked for this trailer to be omitted from every commit.

## What to do

- Write the commit message exactly as drafted, with no auto-appended Cursor/agent attribution trailer.
- The Cursor IDE/agent runtime injects this trailer even when the commit message file does not contain it; passing `--cleanup=verbatim` alone is **not** sufficient to suppress the injection.
- Do **not** add `Co-authored-by:` trailers for any AI agent (Cursor, Claude, Copilot, etc.) unless the user explicitly requests one.
- Human `Co-authored-by:` trailers (for actual collaborating humans) are fine when the user asks for them.

## Proven post-commit fix

Whenever you commit on behalf of the user (and the amend rules in the parent agent prompt permit it — HEAD was authored this conversation and has not been pushed), immediately follow up with:

```bash
git log -1 --format='%B' | grep -v '^Co-authored-by: Cursor ' > /tmp/clean_msg.txt
git commit --amend --no-verify --cleanup=verbatim -F /tmp/clean_msg.txt
```

The `--no-verify` is what bypasses the Cursor trailer injection. `--cleanup=verbatim` preserves the exact message file. After the amend, verify with:

```bash
git log -1 --format='%B' | tail -5
```

If a `Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>` line is still present, do **not** silently leave it — report it to the user.

## Note on already-pushed commits

If the trailer landed on a commit that has already been pushed to the remote, do **not** rewrite history to strip it without an explicit user request. Just stop adding it on subsequent commits.
